  ('mth-121-calculus', 'MTH 121: Functions, Limits, Differentiation & Integration', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Mathematics', '100 Level', 'For f(x) = (x²-25)/(x-5) if x≠5, and f(5)=10, is f continuous at x=5?', '["No — removable discontinuity", "No — jump discontinuity", "No — essential discontinuity", "Yes — f is continuous at x=5"]'::jsonb, 3, '(x²-25)/(x-5) simplifies to x+5 for x≠5, so lim(x→5) f(x) = 10, which equals the given f(5)=10. Since the limit exists and equals f(5), f is continuous at x=5.', 'Continuous Functions', true),
  ('mth-121-calculus', 'MTH 121: Functions, Limits, Differentiation & Integration', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences', 'Mathematics', '100 Level', 'For f(x) = (x²-25)/(x-5) if x≠5, and f(5)=7, is f continuous at x=5?', '["No — essential discontinuity", "Yes — f is continuous at x=5", "No — removable discontinuity", "No — jump discontinuity"]'::jsonb, 2, '(x²-25)/(x-5) simplifies to x+5, so lim(x→5) f(x) = 10, but f(5)=7≠10. Since the limit exists but does not equal f(5), the discontinuity is removable.', 'Types of Discontinuities', true),
